268
00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:34.580
So looking back, absolutely.

269
00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:37.110
Which is natural, that's a natural response.

270
00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:38.330
Natural response.

271
00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.653
But I think looking back,

272
00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:43.730
I'd say almost all of these,

273
00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:48.580
the books that I've written have had some like origin

274
00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:51.073
in the fact that I ran 4:01 and not 3 59.

275
00:11:51.073 --> 00:11:53.829
It's only if sort of premise.

276
00:11:53.829 --> 00:11:58.650
Yeah, and it's not so much that now that I want

277
00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.820
to go like back and do this,

278
00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:02.330
I almost think the best thing that happened

279
00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:04.080
to me was not breaking that barrier

280
00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:07.270
because it forced me to come to terms with something,

281
00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:12.270
which is I was narrowly focused

282
00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:17.590
and my entire world was running and in college,

283
00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:20.950
especially as I was trying to break that barrier,

284
00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:25.690
if a race didn't go, well, I was devastated.

285
00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:27.010
My world was crushed.

286
00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:30.380
There was no separation between identity and performance.

287
00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:31.520
Zero.

288
00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:36.520
It was, if I ran 4:05, it was Steve, you are a failure.

289
00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.883
Like you, yourself are a failure.

290
00:12:41.617 --> 00:12:43.917
And that was really difficult to wrestle with.

291
00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:48.500
And your academic career was really

292
00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:50.540
just an excuse to continue to train.

293
00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:51.790
I've heard you speak about like,

294
00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:54.470
you weren't really all that tapped into whatever you were

295
00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:57.880
doing at school and even graduate school was just basically

296
00:12:57.880 --> 00:12:59.350
a way to get your parents off your back

297
00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:00.630
so you could keep training.

298
00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:02.980
Yeah, so exactly.

299
00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.820
So after I finished my undergrad,

300
00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:07.530
I kind of bummed around for six months

301
00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:10.480
and just like literally bummed around,

302
00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:14.960
like lived off the small savings I had and trained.

303
00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:16.820
And after about six months, my parents were like,

304
00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.600
Steve, you have to do something with your life.

305
00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:23.190
Like, you're smart, you're driven, you gotta do something.

306
00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:25.260
So how did you find your way into coaching?

307
00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.683
So it was pretty much,

308
00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:31.270
I went to grad school so that I could buy myself time

309
00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.420
to keep training and figure out what to do.

310
00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:38.473
And then I found my way into coaching because,

311
00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.540
when I was in right before grad school,

312
00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:44.760
the high school coach I had had retired

313
00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.320
and they didn't have a new coach

314
00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.220
and they were gonna fill a coach,

315
00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:51.800
but they just kind of took their time

316
00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:53.147
as all high school coaches did.

317
00:13:53.147 --> 00:13:54.510
And it was the summer.

318
00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:58.040
And I was back home training, like living with my parents,

319
00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:00.113
bumming around, figuring out what to do.

320
00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.970
And I trained at like one park

321
00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.900
in the suburbs of Houston that everyone trained at.

322
00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.320
And I had all these high school kids who were like,

323
00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:09.380
we have no direction.

324
00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.200
They haven't hired a coach.

325
00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.128
Like, can you help us out?

326
00:14:13.128 --> 00:14:15.180
And I was like, all right, sure.

327
00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:16.850
And there were a bunch of really dedicated

328
00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:19.850
and great kids and helping them out

329
00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:21.600
is what launched me into coaching.

330
00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:23.560
'Cause I was like, this is fun.

331
00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.485
Like I'm getting to give back.

332
00:14:25.485 --> 00:14:28.670
And at that time I had not as much awareness,

333
00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:31.320
but just enough self-awareness where I could be like,

334
00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:33.960
here's a couple really talented kids

335
00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:38.320
who had the talent to make it on the college

336
00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:40.710
and maybe even beyond level.

337
00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:43.500
And maybe I can pass along some wisdom so that

338
00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:46.820
they can like course correct away from the path that I took.

339
00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:48.455
Interesting.

340
00:14:48.455 --> 00:14:51.160
'Cause another response to that,

341
00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:53.050
that I wouldn't judge you for would be like,

342
00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:54.620
I don't want anything to do with running anymore.

343
00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:56.680
Like I didn't achieve my goals

344
00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:57.970
and you could kind of grind on

345
00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.990
that resentment and just move

346
00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:02.300
in a completely different direction.

347
00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:03.431
Yeah

348
00:15:03.431 --> 00:15:07.750
I think that would've been a legitimate option

349
00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:10.140
and I think in some ways running was

350
00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:13.980
the only thing I knew because I just, I did well in school,

351
00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:17.530
but I didn't care at all about anything else.

352
00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:19.840
Nothing else piqued my interest,

353
00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:22.910
the coaching and the kind of exercise science

354
00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:24.950
and psychology piqued my interest probably

355
00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:28.000
out of selfish reasons at first to understand.

356
00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.830
But like that's the only thing where I was like,

357
00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.430
oh, this might be interesting.

358
00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:32.410
Yeah.

359
00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:35.950
So it starts out with you just of your own accord

360
00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.010
and goodwill helping out these high school kids.

361
00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.550
But how does that position you

362
00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:44.982
to end up as this elite guru of track and field?

363
00:15:44.982 --> 00:15:47.110
I honestly,

364
00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:49.449
it's almost like I have no idea

365
00:15:49.449 --> 00:15:50.473
because it happened so fast.

366
00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:55.790
I started coaching high school kids.

367
00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:57.180
And then at the same time I was like,

368
00:15:57.180 --> 00:15:58.220
well, I'm learning a lot.

369
00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:00.700
I'm just gonna put out, like in those days,

370
00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:02.840
like 2010, it was like,

371
00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:05.090
I'm gonna put out a blog online.

372
00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:07.410
And I just start writing about like the science

373
00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:10.030
and I was in grad school and the coaching

374
00:16:10.030 --> 00:16:13.140
and kind of intertwining each

375
00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:15.710
and talking about the lessons learned

376
00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:17.993
and that did fairly well.

377
00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:22.930
And then I think really that started as

378
00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:24.760
I was finishing up grad school.

379
00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:26.700
I was debating whether I should finish early,

380
00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.970
which I could, if I pressed a class

381
00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:33.730
and my thesis or stick around and bum around

382
00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:36.100
for another semester and kind of keep it going

383
00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:39.150
so that I could delay my real life.

384
00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:42.900
And then I get a call out of the blue from Alberto Salazar.

385
00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.410
So you went straight from blogger,

386
00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:48.730
helping out high school kids to Nike Oregon Project.

387
00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:49.563
Yes.

388
00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:53.510
I mean, that doesn't happen.

389
00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:54.343
Right.

390
00:16:54.343 --> 00:16:55.330
Like, that's wild.

391
00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:58.880
And you didn't have any real coaching credentials.

392
00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:00.170
No.

393
00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:01.780
And you weren't a superstar as a runner,

394
00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:03.450
you flamed out in high school.

395
00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:08.450
So what do you think sort of alerted Alberto

396
00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:12.730
that you would be a good candidate to join his project,

397
00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:13.690
which we're gonna get into,

398
00:17:13.690 --> 00:17:17.073
but aside from all the nonsense.

399
00:17:18.239 --> 00:17:22.150
What he told me at the time, and looking back,

400
00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:23.940
I think I'd answer this question differently,

401
00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:26.740
but I'm gonna give you my at the time moment,

402
00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:31.200
is that he read something that I wrote

403
00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:32.190
and he referenced it.

404
00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:33.960
It was like on some

405
00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:37.340
analyzing some training of some elite Ethiopians

406
00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:39.490
and Kenyans and he was like, this is great.

407
00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:42.790
We're looking for an assistant coach

408
00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:46.150
with some science background to come in

409
00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:48.140
and like help me out.

410
00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:49.160
Are you interested?

411
00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:52.040
And I was like 100%.

412
00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:54.174
And to me at that time,

413
00:17:54.174 --> 00:17:58.610
I thought I had hit like the jackpot.

414
00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:03.610
I thought I was like, this was dream job right away.

415
00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:05.830
This is something that I thought maybe,

416
00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:06.990
maybe if I worked hard,

417
00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:08.990
could occur like 20 years in the future.

418
00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:12.513
So I was on all in from all day one.

419
00:18:13.352 --> 00:18:17.050
And so you just pack your bags, move to Eugene, Portland.

420
00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:17.883
Portland. Yeah.

421
00:18:17.883 --> 00:18:20.410
Yes. Portland, sorry.

422
00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:24.193
And hence began this very interesting chapter of your life.

423
00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:28.720
It only lasted about a year and a half,

424
00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:33.090
but it impacted my entire life for the next well,

425
00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:34.470
it's been 10, 11 years.

426
00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:35.590
Right.

427
00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:38.530
So much has been well documented about what happened

428
00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:41.663
with Alberto Salazar and the Nike Oregon Project.

429
00:18:42.519 --> 00:18:44.133
We don't have to rehash all

430
00:18:44.133 --> 00:18:46.320
the dirty details of all of that,

431
00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:49.300
but I do want to give you an opportunity to share your

432
00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:51.420
perspective on what went down.

433
00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:54.250
I mean, you've been interviewed, you've testified,

434
00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:55.790
so there's quite a bit of public

435
00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:57.410
record out there about this.

436
00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:59.880
And as we were chatting before the podcast,

437
00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:00.960
at the same time,

438
00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:04.900
you've done a really good job of not of not allowing

439
00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:08.180
this identity of you as a whistleblower in that context

440
00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:11.380
to define who you are or the work that you do, which,

441
00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:13.220
that befalls a lot of people

442
00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:16.760
who have the audacity or the courage to step out

443
00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:19.060
and publicly denounce something

444
00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:23.440
that they see that is not so good.

445
00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:25.480
Yeah I would,

446
00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:28.000
I think I've handled it that way

447
00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.120
because I was keenly aware of what happens

448
00:19:31.106 --> 00:19:34.050
when your identity is like one thing because of my running.

449
00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:37.200
And I remember thinking about this before

450
00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:40.540
I blew the whistle as like, this is the thing, like,

451
00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:43.830
this could completely define who you are

452
00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:46.470
and you'll have no control over it.

453
00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:51.072
And so I've tried to be like conscious and aware of that.

454
00:19:51.072 --> 00:19:55.800
So yeah, I've tried to be intentional.

455
00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.730
So for people who are perhaps not

456
00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:00.270
that familiar with what happened,

457
00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:04.560
maybe walk through the evolution around you becoming

458
00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.190
aware that things were not as they seem.

459
00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:08.023
Yeah.

460
00:20:08.023 --> 00:20:09.570
So early on, as I said,

461
00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:11.070
I thought it was my dream job,

462
00:20:11.996 --> 00:20:14.180
and I think that's an important framing

463
00:20:15.738 --> 00:20:18.180
because when you go in with that mindset,

464
00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:21.140
what happens is you start to like,

465
00:20:21.140 --> 00:20:23.240
you look on the bright side of everything.

466
00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:28.773
So early on, I'd see some items that I was like,

467
00:20:28.773 --> 00:20:30.803
huh, that's kind of weird.

468
00:20:31.670 --> 00:20:33.720
Like, what would be an example of that.

469
00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:36.390
Very early on,

470
00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:41.180
like I remember it was a couple months after I got there

471
00:20:42.835 --> 00:20:46.640
and one of the athletes had

472
00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:50.623
to use some prescription drug after a race.

473
00:20:51.747 --> 00:20:53.623
And they had said it was asthma related.

474
00:20:55.716 --> 00:20:57.349
And then there was Alberto

475
00:20:57.349 --> 00:20:58.640
started freaking out because then,

476
00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:02.680
well, this drug isn't allowed in competition

477
00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:06.523
and the athlete was about to race again, days later.

478
00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:12.183
So I get a call from Alberto and he says, Steve,

479
00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:18.500
I need you to pick up a sample of pee from this athlete,

480
00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:26.440
fly it to Minnesota, drop it off.

481
00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:31.800
And they're gonna test it to see if it contains the drug.

482
00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:35.410
And this was to transpire days

483
00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:37.090
in advance of a certain race?

484
00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:39.330
Yes, and he was like,

485
00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:43.180
if it doesn't he'll race, if it does, he won't,

486
00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:44.040
we'll pull him out.

487
00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:48.713
And was that drug prescribed at the behest of Salazar?

488
00:21:50.781 --> 00:21:52.170
So this is the other interesting part

489
00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:55.423
that I again realized kind of early on,

490
00:21:56.493 --> 00:21:59.730
but kind of excused away is that a lot of the doctors

491
00:22:02.779 --> 00:22:04.770
who helped the team and then Salazar,

492
00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:05.970
who was in charge of it,

493
00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:09.793
the relationship was like almost intertwined,

494
00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:15.810
where there's several cases where Alberto would be like,

495
00:22:16.370 --> 00:22:20.540
this athlete needs this, which was a prescription drug,

496
00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:23.750
which as a non-doctor like, he shouldn't be able to,

497
00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:24.860
he's not a doctor.

498
00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:25.920
He can't do that.

499
00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:29.640
Or there'd be cases at the track where he'd be like,

500
00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:31.570
here, you need to take this.

501
00:22:31.570 --> 00:22:34.280
And then he'd hand someone,

502
00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:38.590
a prescription bottle of someone else or give someone else

503
00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:41.410
like a prescription inhaler that didn't belong

504
00:22:41.410 --> 00:22:44.030
to them that wasn't prescribed to them.

505
00:22:44.030 --> 00:22:46.850
So it was like a bunch of this,

506
00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:50.883
these things that push beyond like the normal,

507
00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:56.220
doctor, patient, like workings.

508
00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:58.340
And you're just making mental notes

509
00:22:59.608 --> 00:23:00.770
of these little incidents

510
00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:04.440
that are happening out in the open.

511
00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:08.323
Relatively within the ecosystem of this team?

512
00:23:09.243 --> 00:23:11.130
Right, and that's kind of what it was.

513
00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:15.070
And then I think the first time my alarm bell went off is I

514
00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:20.070
was looking through a scientific research report done by

515
00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:23.140
the man who was at formerly

516
00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:25.213
the head of the Nike Sports Science Lab.

517
00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:27.960
And I'm just perusing through it.

518
00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:29.793
It's on like altitude training.

519
00:23:31.786 --> 00:23:36.493
And I see this, the doctor's notes, the scientist notes.

520
00:23:37.471 --> 00:23:39.560
And it says under Galen Rupp's name,

521
00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:44.363
it says presently on testosterone medication.

522
00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:49.880
And as someone who's not esteemed and not a doctor,

523
00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:54.800
but I know when testosterone medication comes in, I'm like.

524
00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:55.937
You're like, huh.

525
00:23:55.937 --> 00:23:57.710
Huh. This is weird.

526
00:23:57.710 --> 00:23:59.810
So what do you do with that information?

527
00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:04.480
I mean, initially I called my parents for advice.

528
00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:08.730
I'll be honest, 'cause I was like, I don't know what to do.

529
00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:09.610
This is strange.

530
00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:11.743
Like I know testosterone's illegal.

531
00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:14.270
Like what do I do?

532
00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:16.680
And they're like, well you gotta confront Alberto.

