That's what I had to wrap my head around.

1057
00:48:53.630 --> 00:48:56.890
And we talked at the beginning, I've had four books,

1058
00:48:56.890 --> 00:48:59.870
the first book, "Science of Running",

1059
00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:01.143
which was self-published.

1060
00:49:01.990 --> 00:49:06.960
I put it out there right before the original

1061
00:49:06.960 --> 00:49:08.990
like piece was supposed to come out.

1062
00:49:08.990 --> 00:49:10.130
As a prophylactic.

1063
00:49:10.130 --> 00:49:11.420
Like just so you know,

1064
00:49:11.420 --> 00:49:12.580
before I head into this,

1065
00:49:12.580 --> 00:49:14.700
I actually know what I'm talking about with running.

1066
00:49:14.700 --> 00:49:15.900
And maybe there'd be some kind

1067
00:49:15.900 --> 00:49:17.780
of insurance policy for your career.

1068
00:49:17.780 --> 00:49:19.030
Exactly.

1069
00:49:19.030 --> 00:49:23.167
So that I had something else, financially, expertise-wise,

1070
00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:27.320
something else that I was like,

1071
00:49:27.320 --> 00:49:30.083
okay, if this all goes to hell in a hand basket,

1072
00:49:31.170 --> 00:49:32.910
I'll be okay for a while to figure out

1073
00:49:32.910 --> 00:49:34.123
what the next path is.

1074
00:49:35.330 --> 00:49:36.550
How bad did it get?

1075
00:49:36.550 --> 00:49:39.240
I mean, did they have people following you?

1076
00:49:39.240 --> 00:49:41.510
Were they trying to dig up dirt on you

1077
00:49:41.510 --> 00:49:45.200
and publishing false accounts in the press of who you were?

1078
00:49:45.200 --> 00:49:48.043
Like, what was your lived experience of that period?

1079
00:49:48.960 --> 00:49:52.483
I mean, it was, I had to get off the internet,

1080
00:49:53.710 --> 00:49:56.211
which is much harder than you realize it is.

1081
00:49:56.211 --> 00:49:57.763
In the modern world,

1082
00:49:58.853 --> 00:50:02.050
but it was driving me nuts because yeah,

1083
00:50:02.050 --> 00:50:03.184
there'd be a bunch of-

1084
00:50:03.184 --> 00:50:04.098
You're getting dragged.

1085
00:50:04.098 --> 00:50:06.110
You're getting dragged in the press, getting dragged,

1086
00:50:06.110 --> 00:50:08.623
wherever I'd show up to track meets.

1087
00:50:09.658 --> 00:50:13.360
And literally other coaches would be like, hey Steve,

1088
00:50:13.360 --> 00:50:16.373
like good to see you, but I don't want to be near you.

1089
00:50:17.340 --> 00:50:19.120
And I'd be like the ostracized,

1090
00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:20.860
like person over in the corner

1091
00:50:22.697 --> 00:50:25.550
because Nike's the biggest thing in track and field.

1092
00:50:25.550 --> 00:50:26.920
And it was, I mean,

1093
00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:30.720
I had the FBI show up at my house.

1094
00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:34.440
I had reporters literally stalk me

1095
00:50:34.440 --> 00:50:37.686
at my house and at my work.

1096
00:50:37.686 --> 00:50:38.519
That's so crazy.

1097
00:50:38.519 --> 00:50:41.819
All leading up to of course,

1098
00:50:41.819 --> 00:50:44.170
this pinnacle moment of you testifying

1099
00:50:44.170 --> 00:50:46.483
with Alberto sitting right there.

1100
00:50:48.460 --> 00:50:50.540
It's the hardest thing I've ever done

1101
00:50:50.540 --> 00:50:52.380
was not blowing the whistle.

1102
00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:54.080
It was sitting there in that room.

1103
00:50:57.219 --> 00:51:00.173
I thought I knew kind of what a panic attack was,

1104
00:51:02.060 --> 00:51:04.160
but I didn't know until experiencing that.

1105
00:51:05.180 --> 00:51:08.460
'Cause I remember sitting there being like,

1106
00:51:08.460 --> 00:51:11.223
oh my dear Lord, like,

1107
00:51:13.050 --> 00:51:15.700
I'm about to like have to share everything,

1108
00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:17.640
get grilled, et cetera.

1109
00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:19.440
They're gonna try and tear me apart.

1110
00:51:22.397 --> 00:51:23.230
And it was nerve-wracking.

1111
00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:25.820
Like I'm an introvert by nature.

1112
00:51:25.820 --> 00:51:27.483
I like to write.

1113
00:51:28.744 --> 00:51:30.750
Like I do get up and talk,

1114
00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:35.260
but it's not my thing.

1115
00:51:35.260 --> 00:51:38.010
So it was the worst experience of my life.

1116
00:51:38.010 --> 00:51:40.490
I wouldn't wish anybody else to have that.

1117
00:51:40.490 --> 00:51:41.650
Yeah, to have him sitting there.

1118
00:51:41.650 --> 00:51:44.950
I mean, was he just steely eyeing you the whole time

1119
00:51:45.942 --> 00:51:48.120
and what was the experience of being cross-examined

1120
00:51:48.120 --> 00:51:49.290
in that context?

1121
00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:50.310
Well, horrible.

1122
00:51:50.310 --> 00:51:52.610
Yeah. I mean, they're just staring you down

1123
00:51:55.340 --> 00:51:59.830
and trying to like poke any sort of hole, twist,

1124
00:51:59.830 --> 00:52:04.830
any sort of thing you've done or said at any moment

1125
00:52:04.950 --> 00:52:07.953
in basically the history of your life.

1126
00:52:09.933 --> 00:52:12.650
It's throwing whatever sticks at the wall

1127
00:52:12.650 --> 00:52:14.010
and here you are in your head.

1128
00:52:14.010 --> 00:52:16.640
You're like, that didn't happen.

1129
00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:19.810
Like what in the world are you talking about?

1130
00:52:19.810 --> 00:52:23.663
Like, this is BS, but like you,

1131
00:52:25.017 --> 00:52:29.367
all you can do is say that and they try and confuse

1132
00:52:30.650 --> 00:52:34.650
everything because you gotta remember at the time

1133
00:52:34.650 --> 00:52:37.260
of this stuff, it's all stuff that happened

1134
00:52:37.260 --> 00:52:40.550
five, six, seven, by the end of it, nine years ago,

1135
00:52:40.550 --> 00:52:41.383
10 years ago.

1136
00:52:44.182 --> 00:52:47.127
And you only actually saw a couple incidents.

1137
00:52:48.486 --> 00:52:50.470
So it's not like you were down in the basement

1138
00:52:50.470 --> 00:52:54.190
with whatever doctor he was working with and really had

1139
00:52:54.190 --> 00:52:58.240
hands on firsthand accounts of all kinds of stuff.

1140
00:52:58.240 --> 00:53:00.550
So you could see how they could pick

1141
00:53:00.550 --> 00:53:02.860
that apart and create doubt.

1142
00:53:02.860 --> 00:53:03.693
Exactly.

1143
00:53:03.693 --> 00:53:07.560
It's not like I had the smoking gun where I saw someone

1144
00:53:07.560 --> 00:53:09.800
like inject themselves with a steroid

1145
00:53:09.800 --> 00:53:11.000
and took a picture of it.

1146
00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:13.430
Like I didn't do that.

1147
00:53:13.430 --> 00:53:17.060
So because it was so nuanced.

1148
00:53:17.060 --> 00:53:17.893
And because again,

1149
00:53:17.893 --> 00:53:22.390
you're looking at people in the panel who maybe you don't

1150
00:53:22.390 --> 00:53:25.470
have the expertise to understand what high sport

1151
00:53:25.470 --> 00:53:28.790
and the science behind it and all this stuff.

1152
00:53:28.790 --> 00:53:31.000
And what is illegal and what isn't.

1153
00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:31.833
Exactly.

1154
00:53:31.833 --> 00:53:34.703
And what's normal and what's not normal in sport.

1155
00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:39.310
And that was really hard

1156
00:53:39.310 --> 00:53:41.670
because I'm sitting here being like, no,

1157
00:53:41.670 --> 00:53:44.390
you guys like, this is crazy.

1158
00:53:44.390 --> 00:53:45.960
This is not normal.

1159
00:53:45.960 --> 00:53:48.183
Like this doesn't occur anywhere else,

1160
00:53:49.439 --> 00:53:50.940
but like to convey that is something else.

1161
00:53:50.940 --> 00:53:51.850
Right.

1162
00:53:51.850 --> 00:53:55.100
Well, you did the thing and what happened happened.

1163
00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:56.230
And in the wake of that,

1164
00:53:56.230 --> 00:54:01.230
have you had any direct correspondence with Salazar?

1165
00:54:01.410 --> 00:54:02.367
Like has he reached out to you?

1166
00:54:02.367 --> 00:54:04.233
Has he tried to contact you?

1167
00:54:05.120 --> 00:54:06.560
No, no.

1168
00:54:06.560 --> 00:54:09.390
I mean those guys, never.

1169
00:54:09.390 --> 00:54:11.260
And what about when you

1170
00:54:11.260 --> 00:54:14.863
bump into Nike people at track meets and things like that?

1171
00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:18.340
I mean, before all the verdicts came,

1172
00:54:18.340 --> 00:54:20.640
they'd try and intimidate me.

1173
00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:22.260
But have they clean house since then,

1174
00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:24.976
so it's new and different people?

1175
00:54:24.976 --> 00:54:29.260
They have for the most part, not entirely,

1176
00:54:29.260 --> 00:54:32.803
but a lot of them are different and gone.

1177
00:54:33.870 --> 00:54:37.810
So it's just strange, honestly.

1178
00:54:37.810 --> 00:54:40.223
Because in my head I'm sitting there being like,

1179
00:54:41.253 --> 00:54:43.190
look, I'm not trying to ruin anyone's life or do anything.

1180
00:54:43.190 --> 00:54:45.090
Like, I'm just telling you what I saw.

1181
00:54:47.400 --> 00:54:50.080
Right, and with that,

1182
00:54:50.080 --> 00:54:52.570
with having had that experience

1183
00:54:52.570 --> 00:54:55.210
and then coaching at the elite

1184
00:54:55.210 --> 00:54:58.390
collegiate level and being at all these meets

1185
00:54:58.390 --> 00:55:00.870
and knowing these athletes firsthand,

1186
00:55:00.870 --> 00:55:03.620
when you're watching the Olympic trials

1187
00:55:03.620 --> 00:55:07.820
or some big track meet and you see exceptional performances,

1188
00:55:07.820 --> 00:55:09.550
like what goes on in your mind?

1189
00:55:09.550 --> 00:55:13.253
Like, we all wanna believe that sport is clean,

1190
00:55:14.440 --> 00:55:18.230
but you knowing better than anyone

1191
00:55:18.230 --> 00:55:21.740
what constitutes a legitimate breakthrough versus

1192
00:55:21.740 --> 00:55:23.010
an enhanced breakthrough?

1193
00:55:23.010 --> 00:55:24.480
Like, are you cynical?

1194
00:55:24.480 --> 00:55:28.330
Like what is your lens on on sport?

1195
00:55:28.330 --> 00:55:30.260
Not just in track and field, but like

1196
00:55:30.260 --> 00:55:31.190
we could talk about psych.

1197
00:55:31.190 --> 00:55:32.530
We could talk about any of the sports

1198
00:55:32.530 --> 00:55:34.130
in which this is rampant.

1199
00:55:34.130 --> 00:55:35.823
and there's been controversies.

1200
00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:41.960
For a while I was very cynical and I realized

1201
00:55:43.118 --> 00:55:45.200
that it was killing my love of something

1202
00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:48.760
that I really enjoy doing, participating in,

1203
00:55:48.760 --> 00:55:49.703
helping out in,

1204
00:55:51.759 --> 00:55:53.950
and where I've come to now

1205
00:55:55.240 --> 00:55:59.920
is I cheer on the folks

1206
00:55:59.920 --> 00:56:02.480
and the coaches and the athletes

1207
00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:03.900
who I feel really good about

1208
00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:07.790
who I think are doing things the right way.

1209
00:56:07.790 --> 00:56:11.420
And I can get maybe not to 100% certainty,

1210
00:56:11.420 --> 00:56:13.260
but I can get close.

1211
00:56:13.260 --> 00:56:15.530
And those are the people I watch.

1212
00:56:15.530 --> 00:56:17.500
I cheer for, et cetera.

1213
00:56:17.500 --> 00:56:20.820
If you're not in that group, you set a record.

1214
00:56:20.820 --> 00:56:21.852
I don't care.

1215
00:56:21.852 --> 00:56:24.800
And I'm not gonna ask you to name those people,

1216
00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:27.670
but clearly I could tell there's certain individuals

1217
00:56:27.670 --> 00:56:31.430
where you're like, okay, right.

1218
00:56:31.430 --> 00:56:34.600
Yeah, and it's hard-

1219
00:56:34.600 --> 00:56:37.750
With that, it has to be, I mean, it's a choice,

1220
00:56:37.750 --> 00:56:39.470
but it still has to be a challenge

1221
00:56:39.470 --> 00:56:42.120
for you to remain enthusiastic about the sport.

1222
00:56:42.120 --> 00:56:42.953
It does.

1223
00:56:43.859 --> 00:56:45.560
And the hardest part to me though,

1224
00:56:45.560 --> 00:56:48.900
is that there were a lot of people,

1225
00:56:48.900 --> 00:56:50.660
I kind of thought maybe this was naive.

1226
00:56:50.660 --> 00:56:53.240
I thought, okay, I'm gonna blow the whistle.

1227
00:56:53.240 --> 00:56:54.390
People will see all this.

1228
00:56:54.390 --> 00:56:58.370
If it comes out, the verdict did in the favor of

1229
00:56:58.370 --> 00:57:00.973
we were telling the truth, that well,

1230
00:57:01.860 --> 00:57:04.420
people would see and they'd have like ethics

1231
00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:06.640
and morals and back the good side.

1232
00:57:06.640 --> 00:57:09.510
And like, but you still see people

1233
00:57:09.510 --> 00:57:14.510
who go to like train with similar athletes

1234
00:57:14.920 --> 00:57:17.410
or coaches as were part of the Oregon Project,

1235
00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:21.680
or like kind of mesh in that environment.

1236
00:57:21.680 --> 00:57:24.140
And in my head, I'm like, like guys,

1237
00:57:24.140 --> 00:57:26.410
you know, you've heard my story.

1238
00:57:26.410 --> 00:57:27.380
You've seen the report.

1239
00:57:27.380 --> 00:57:29.810
You've seen what Mary Kane went through

1240
00:57:29.810 --> 00:57:33.240
and Kara Goucher and there are people like,

1241
00:57:33.240 --> 00:57:35.410
ah, like, oh no, like,

1242
00:57:35.410 --> 00:57:37.300
even though you're like coach an athlete

1243
00:57:37.300 --> 00:57:38.950
at that time, like, I don't care.

1244
00:57:38.950 --> 00:57:40.787
Like we'll just blow this off.

1245
00:57:40.787 --> 00:57:42.390
And that drives me nuts.

1246
00:57:42.390 --> 00:57:44.230
But you can also have compassion for them.

1247
00:57:44.230 --> 00:57:46.930
They're young people who are trying to have a professional

1248
00:57:46.930 --> 00:57:49.750
career in a sport where there's very little money

1249
00:57:49.750 --> 00:57:53.430
and there is still a level of prestige attached

1250
00:57:53.430 --> 00:57:55.990
to Nike and Oregon and all of that.

1251
00:57:55.990 --> 00:57:59.280
And you know, far be it from us to judge somebody

1252
00:57:59.280 --> 00:58:02.100
who kind of needs that in their life.

1253
00:58:02.100 --> 00:58:05.790
And has to find some way to look past

1254
00:58:05.790 --> 00:58:10.580
past transgressions for the sake of their future career.

1255
00:58:10.580 --> 00:58:13.010
I 100% get that.

1256
00:58:13.010 --> 00:58:18.010
And I think what it tells me or what I've realized

1257
00:58:19.920 --> 00:58:24.000
is that I was very,

1258
00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:27.570
we all come at it from like a naive perspective

1259
00:58:27.570 --> 00:58:29.800
and you tend to think like ethics and morals

1260
00:58:29.800 --> 00:58:32.020
and all those things are like hard grained like

1261
00:58:32.020 --> 00:58:33.550
in you and what have you.

1262
00:58:33.550 --> 00:58:35.680
But I've realized is environment impacts you more

1263
00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:39.090
than you realize, more than we count for.

1264
00:58:39.090 --> 00:58:44.090
So I don't, I try my best, not to blame people,

1265
00:58:44.180 --> 00:58:46.850
not to be like, oh, why are you doing that?

1266
00:58:46.850 --> 00:58:48.440
Why did you choose that decision?

1267
00:58:48.440 --> 00:58:50.610
Right, but you can reflect on your own decisions

1268
00:58:50.610 --> 00:58:53.649
and why you made them at that time.

1269
00:58:53.649 --> 00:58:56.440
And you know, that I'm sure allows you to better understand.

1270
00:58:56.440 --> 00:58:57.273
Exactly.

1271
00:58:57.273 --> 00:59:00.690
So I try to have nuance there where it's like,

1272
00:59:00.690 --> 00:59:02.180
the world is not good and evil.

1273
00:59:02.180 --> 00:59:03.690
It's not black and white.

1274
00:59:03.690 --> 00:59:07.790
We're all capable of all sorts of good and bad decisions.

1275
00:59:07.790 --> 00:59:11.330
And that's just reality.

1276
00:59:11.330 --> 00:59:12.480
Right.

1277
00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:14.820
And I would imagine in the way that you look back

1278
00:59:14.820 --> 00:59:19.430
on that 4:01 mile and your inability

1279
00:59:19.430 --> 00:59:23.590
to break that barrier as a gift that allowed you

1280
00:59:23.590 --> 00:59:25.350
to become something greater,

1281
00:59:25.350 --> 00:59:27.570
you can reflect back on that experience

1282
00:59:29.210 --> 00:59:34.210
at Oregon Project and think, but not for that,

1283
00:59:34.500 --> 00:59:35.940
you would've become the coach

1284
00:59:35.940 --> 00:59:37.480
that you became at Houston.

1285
00:59:37.480 --> 00:59:38.870
Like you could've just remained

1286
00:59:38.870 --> 00:59:41.770
under Salazar's wing forever and never fully developed

1287
00:59:41.770 --> 00:59:45.000
your own identity as a head coach.

1288
00:59:45.000 --> 00:59:48.700
It shaped how I see not only like coaching,

1289
00:59:48.700 --> 00:59:49.890
but also life.

1290
00:59:49.890 --> 00:59:51.680
Like what is really important here.

1291
00:59:51.680 --> 00:59:52.900
Exactly.

1292
00:59:52.900 --> 00:59:56.963
If you asked 25 year old Steve, he would say,

1293
00:59:58.028 --> 01:00:01.028
oh, like Olympic medals running around really fast in ovals,

1294
01:00:01.980 --> 01:00:04.423
if you ask me after this experience,

1295
01:00:05.593 --> 01:00:08.060
it's no, it's, I want healthy,

1296
01:00:08.060 --> 01:00:12.840
happy human beings who can use sport as a way to challenge

1297
01:00:12.840 --> 01:00:15.047
and push boundaries and like find themselves

1298
01:00:15.047 --> 01:00:17.460
and like struggle in all those things.

1299
01:00:17.460 --> 01:00:18.930
But we can do it in a way

1300
01:00:18.930 --> 01:00:21.140
that like makes them grow as individuals.

1301
01:00:21.140 --> 01:00:22.120
And if we do that, like,

1302
01:00:22.120 --> 01:00:25.170
who cares if you run 4:01 or 3:55.

1303
01:00:25.170 --> 01:00:26.660
And that mission statement

1304
01:00:26.660 --> 01:00:28.330
then begets all these questions.

1305
01:00:28.330 --> 01:00:32.280
Well, if that's the priority, how do we make healthy,

1306
01:00:32.280 --> 01:00:36.510
happy athletes who can thrive in a sustainable way?

1307
01:00:36.510 --> 01:00:38.400
Hence all of these books that you've written

1308
01:00:38.400 --> 01:00:39.770
and all the research that you've done

1309
01:00:39.770 --> 01:00:42.040
and all of that, which is fundamentally

1310
01:00:42.040 --> 01:00:42.890
why we're here today.

1311
01:00:42.890 --> 01:00:44.315
Like, I didn't know,

1312
01:00:44.315 --> 01:00:46.560
we were gonna spend a whole hour on Nike Oregon Project,

1313
01:00:46.560 --> 01:00:49.610
but thank you for being so candorous about that

1314
01:00:49.610 --> 01:00:52.393
because I think there is still this,

1315
01:00:53.341 --> 01:00:54.590
it's interesting,

1316
01:00:54.590 --> 01:00:56.010
we were talking before the podcast, like,

1317
01:00:56.010 --> 01:00:57.360
it's the story that doesn't go away.

1318
01:00:57.360 --> 01:01:00.349
Like, even though you're like 2012,

1319
01:01:00.349 --> 01:01:01.240
but yet we're still talking about this

1320
01:01:01.240 --> 01:01:03.490
because it kind of continues to evolve.

1321
01:01:03.490 --> 01:01:06.260
And it also is a basis point for how we think

1322
01:01:06.260 --> 01:01:10.480
about other controversies that are similar or analogous.

1323
01:01:10.480 --> 01:01:12.930
And it, like, won't leave you on some level,

1324
01:01:12.930 --> 01:01:16.150
despite your desire to put it in the rear view mirror,

1325
01:01:16.150 --> 01:01:19.160
but I appreciate you being open about it.

1326
01:01:19.160 --> 01:01:23.055
But let's turn this to, to the new book,

1327
01:01:23.055 --> 01:01:24.706
"Do Hard Things" and kind

1328
01:01:24.706 --> 01:01:26.340
of what you're really interested in,

1329
01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:28.810
which is this idea of how do we cultivate

1330
01:01:30.022 --> 01:01:32.840
not just great athletes who are resilient

1331
01:01:32.840 --> 01:01:36.130
and capable of optimizing performance,

1332
01:01:36.130 --> 01:01:39.290
but ultimately good human beings

1333
01:01:39.290 --> 01:01:40.720
and the principles that you've learned

1334
01:01:40.720 --> 01:01:43.530
as a coach that translate into the workplace

1335
01:01:43.530 --> 01:01:45.480
and into our personal lives, et cetera.

1336
01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:48.760
So maybe a good place to start while

1337
01:01:48.760 --> 01:01:50.100
we're on the subject of running.

1338
01:01:50.100 --> 01:01:53.870
And the 4:01 mile is really

1339
01:01:53.870 --> 01:01:55.630
what appears to be an impetus for this book,

1340
01:01:55.630 --> 01:01:57.950
which is this other mile that you were running,

1341
01:01:57.950 --> 01:02:00.080
where you had this vocal chord incident

1342
01:02:00.080 --> 01:02:04.630
that made you rethink your approach to stress

1343
01:02:04.630 --> 01:02:07.430
and your approach to toughness in general.

1344
01:02:07.430 --> 01:02:08.390
Yeah. Yeah.

1345
01:02:08.390 --> 01:02:10.709
So this happened when I was in college

1346
01:02:10.709 --> 01:02:14.690
I was in the middle of a mile

1347
01:02:14.690 --> 01:02:16.470
at Cal Berkeley running against

1348
01:02:16.470 --> 01:02:17.970
some really good runners trying to break

1349
01:02:17.970 --> 01:02:20.693
the four minute barrier, was running really well.

1350
01:02:21.826 --> 01:02:25.330
And then all of a sudden after three laps

1351
01:02:25.330 --> 01:02:28.033
or so I couldn't breathe.

1352
01:02:30.050 --> 01:02:31.680
And when I say I couldn't breathe,

1353
01:02:31.680 --> 01:02:34.283
like I literally could not get air in.

1354
01:02:36.136 --> 01:02:38.530
And it sent me like, what in the hell is going on?

1355
01:02:38.530 --> 01:02:40.090
I collapse on the ground

1356
01:02:41.600 --> 01:02:43.878
I'm trying to figure out how to breathe.

1357
01:02:43.878 --> 01:02:45.858
And the way you describe it,

1358
01:02:45.858 --> 01:02:47.285
sorry to step over your words.

1359
01:02:47.285 --> 01:02:49.250
But the way you describe it's not

1360
01:02:49.250 --> 01:02:52.980
that you were out of your depth in terms of exertion,

1361
01:02:52.980 --> 01:02:54.340
like you were pacing yourself

1362
01:02:54.340 --> 01:02:55.810
and you felt like you were,

1363
01:02:55.810 --> 01:02:57.170
hitting the mark that you needed

1364
01:02:57.170 --> 01:02:58.900
to hit at that stage of the race.

1365
01:02:58.900 --> 01:03:01.249
I think you had a lap and a half left

1366
01:03:01.249 --> 01:03:02.309
or something like that.

1367
01:03:02.309 --> 01:03:03.570
And you're like, I'm right where I need to be.

1368
01:03:03.570 --> 01:03:04.640
So it wasn't like, oh,

1369
01:03:04.640 --> 01:03:08.150
I've just over exerted myself or misjudged my pace.

1370
01:03:08.150 --> 01:03:10.090
It was something altogether different.

1371
01:03:10.090 --> 01:03:15.090
Yes, it was as if someone had like shoved something

1372
01:03:16.110 --> 01:03:18.543
down my throat and I was choking.

1373
01:03:19.400 --> 01:03:20.870
That's the best way I can describe it.

1374
01:03:20.870 --> 01:03:23.870
Not like there was just nothing

1375
01:03:23.870 --> 01:03:26.773
that could get in or out of that passage way.

1376
01:03:28.130 --> 01:03:31.230
And what it turned out to be after a lot of testing

1377
01:03:32.158 --> 01:03:36.010
is that it was this thing called vocal cord dysfunction,

1378
01:03:36.010 --> 01:03:38.060
which is essentially what happens,

1379
01:03:38.060 --> 01:03:39.680
or what normally happens is

1380
01:03:40.520 --> 01:03:42.640
your vocal chords open when you breathe

1381
01:03:42.640 --> 01:03:43.760
and shut when you don't.

1382
01:03:43.760 --> 01:03:46.191
And they just kind of go back and forth.

1383
01:03:46.191 --> 01:03:47.690
For whatever reason,

1384
01:03:47.690 --> 01:03:50.140
my vocal chords almost flipped where

1385
01:03:50.140 --> 01:03:53.440
the stress signal told them to shut

1386
01:03:53.440 --> 01:03:56.090
when they should be opened.

1387
01:03:56.090 --> 01:03:58.670
And it's this really weird kind of disorder

1388
01:03:58.670 --> 01:04:00.290
that is more common than we think,

1389
01:04:00.290 --> 01:04:02.970
but it often gets confused with asthma,

1390
01:04:02.970 --> 01:04:05.723
even though it's like the opposite effect.

1391
01:04:09.179 --> 01:04:11.210
So what happens and what the research tells you

1392
01:04:11.210 --> 01:04:14.590
is that it generally happens

1393
01:04:14.590 --> 01:04:18.920
in driven perfectionist type individuals

1394
01:04:18.920 --> 01:04:21.730
who for whatever reason have

1395
01:04:21.730 --> 01:04:26.050
some sort of trigger that like just almost makes

1396
01:04:26.050 --> 01:04:30.753
the brain go haywire and have this opposite reaction.

1397
01:04:31.790 --> 01:04:34.420
And what I had to learn to do from that is I was so

1398
01:04:34.420 --> 01:04:38.080
accustomed to being like, well, how do you handle pain?

1399
01:04:38.080 --> 01:04:39.710
You just push through, you dig deeper.

1400
01:04:39.710 --> 01:04:42.430
You like grit your teeth and like put your head down

1401
01:04:42.430 --> 01:04:44.757
and like go and push for it.

1402
01:04:44.757 --> 01:04:47.850
And that would no longer work because if I did anything

1403
01:04:47.850 --> 01:04:50.320
that caused any sort of anxiousness

1404
01:04:50.320 --> 01:04:54.610
or tension in me that became a trigger

1405
01:04:54.610 --> 01:04:57.230
for my vocal chords, literally shutting,

1406
01:04:57.230 --> 01:04:59.630
and then I'd have to drop out and figure out

1407
01:04:59.630 --> 01:05:01.125
how to breathe again.

1408
01:05:01.125 --> 01:05:04.800
So you're forced into this situation where you have

1409
01:05:04.800 --> 01:05:08.380
to figure out a different way to avoid that response,

1410
01:05:08.380 --> 01:05:12.140
which is gonna require an antithetical relationship

1411
01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:14.400
with pushing through pain

1412
01:05:14.400 --> 01:05:17.920
and more of a Eastern philosophical,

1413
01:05:17.920 --> 01:05:21.120
kind of letting go surrender, mindfulness

1414
01:05:22.570 --> 01:05:26.420
driven relationship with sport in order

1415
01:05:26.420 --> 01:05:30.000
to avoid having that kind of an incident.

1416
01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:30.833
Exactly.

1417
01:05:30.833 --> 01:05:34.300
And for someone who grew up again in the south

1418
01:05:34.300 --> 01:05:37.220
around like football and sport,

1419
01:05:37.220 --> 01:05:41.150
that Eastern philosophy didn't come naturally.

1420
01:05:41.150 --> 01:05:42.260
Yeah.

1421
01:05:42.260 --> 01:05:43.093
Well, it's just,

1422
01:05:43.093 --> 01:05:46.390
it's not even like part of the parlance and you get into

1423
01:05:46.390 --> 01:05:50.950
this in the book around kind of our unhealthy, but you know,

1424
01:05:50.950 --> 01:05:54.650
conventional ideas around what it means to be tough

1425
01:05:54.650 --> 01:05:58.400
in sport and these legendary stories of these mythic

1426
01:05:58.400 --> 01:06:02.420
coach figures and athlete figures that have cemented this

1427
01:06:02.420 --> 01:06:05.410
notion in our consciousness to the point

1428
01:06:05.410 --> 01:06:07.650
where we don't even question it.

1429
01:06:07.650 --> 01:06:09.890
Yeah, it's kind of wild.

1430
01:06:09.890 --> 01:06:12.940
It's like, I like to think like every society

1431
01:06:12.940 --> 01:06:14.920
has their and culture has their kind

1432
01:06:14.920 --> 01:06:17.370
of like master narratives, in the US,

1433
01:06:17.370 --> 01:06:19.070
it's like, oh, the American dream,

1434
01:06:19.070 --> 01:06:20.220
like do all this thing,

1435
01:06:21.358 --> 01:06:25.900
in sport, in toughness, in military and all this stuff.

1436
01:06:25.900 --> 01:06:30.631
The master narrative is one of like push through,

1437
01:06:30.631 --> 01:06:32.283
ignore the pain.

1438
01:06:33.412 --> 01:06:35.430
Like don't cry, don't show any emotions.

1439
01:06:35.430 --> 01:06:37.340
Whatever doesn't kill you makes you stronger.

1440
01:06:37.340 --> 01:06:38.173
Exactly.

1441
01:06:39.150 --> 01:06:41.830
And you have story after story after story.

1442
01:06:41.830 --> 01:06:44.090
And what's interesting is you deconstruct these like

1443
01:06:44.090 --> 01:06:47.030
beginning with Bear Bryant and him arriving

1444
01:06:47.030 --> 01:06:49.780
at Texas A&amp;M and this legendary

1445
01:06:49.780 --> 01:06:53.150
fable around how he pushed these kids

1446
01:06:53.150 --> 01:06:56.283
in their summer training camp, leading into the fall,

1447
01:06:57.921 --> 01:06:59.920
to the point where like most of them quit.

1448
01:06:59.920 --> 01:07:01.630
But then two years later they become

1449
01:07:01.630 --> 01:07:03.010
this reigning champion team.

1450
01:07:03.010 --> 01:07:06.130
And the kind of mythos of Bear Bryant is born.

1451
01:07:06.130 --> 01:07:08.500
And when you deconstruct it, you realize, well,

1452
01:07:08.500 --> 01:07:09.580
this is all horseshit,

1453
01:07:09.580 --> 01:07:12.240
like this isn't even actually what happened.

1454
01:07:12.240 --> 01:07:13.073
Yeah.

1455
01:07:13.073 --> 01:07:14.770
That was one of my favorite.

1456
01:07:14.770 --> 01:07:17.050
My wife might actually be upset with me

1457
01:07:17.050 --> 01:07:19.120
'cause she's a Texas A&amp;M grad.

1458
01:07:19.120 --> 01:07:20.940
So it's like part of their mythos.

1459
01:07:20.940 --> 01:07:25.940
But that, it's famous, especially in Texas,

1460
01:07:26.450 --> 01:07:28.310
like junction boys is what it's called.

1461
01:07:28.310 --> 01:07:29.520
Like go to the camp,

1462
01:07:29.520 --> 01:07:31.200
do all this crazy stuff.

1463
01:07:31.200 --> 01:07:32.390
This is how they got better.

1464
01:07:32.390 --> 01:07:33.980
This is how they became

1465
01:07:33.980 --> 01:07:35.540
the team that was legendary.

1466
01:07:35.540 --> 01:07:38.400
And the reality is the year they go to the camp,

1467
01:07:38.400 --> 01:07:39.760
they do all this crazy stuff.

1468
01:07:39.760 --> 01:07:41.053
They went one in nine,

1469
01:07:42.360 --> 01:07:43.630
the year they were good,

1470
01:07:43.630 --> 01:07:46.763
only a handful of the players were actually left.

1471
01:07:48.282 --> 01:07:50.920
What had happened is they'd gotten better players.

1472
01:07:50.920 --> 01:07:51.753
How?

1473
01:07:51.753 --> 01:07:53.956
Bryant admitted himself,

1474
01:07:53.956 --> 01:07:56.750
like they paid to recruit better players to Texas A&amp;M.

1475
01:07:56.750 --> 01:07:58.470
But also the marketing hype

1476
01:07:58.470 --> 01:08:00.580
of that story made it attractive.

1477
01:08:00.580 --> 01:08:01.803
Exactly.

1478
01:08:01.803 --> 01:08:03.880
So you can create

1479
01:08:03.880 --> 01:08:07.790
this magnetic field out of this legend,

1480
01:08:07.790 --> 01:08:11.910
but the truth of it was that like a lot of the,

1481
01:08:11.910 --> 01:08:14.910
like the freshmen, by the time they were seniors,

1482
01:08:14.910 --> 01:08:17.330
who had been there during that period of time,

1483
01:08:17.330 --> 01:08:20.163
the stars opted out of the camp.

1484
01:08:21.260 --> 01:08:22.560
And some of the guys that quit the camp

1485
01:08:22.560 --> 01:08:24.870
ended up being superstars in other fields.

1486
01:08:24.870 --> 01:08:26.450
Like they were amazing individuals

1487
01:08:26.450 --> 01:08:30.140
had they been sort of mentored in a healthier,

1488
01:08:30.140 --> 01:08:31.320
more appropriate way.

1489
01:08:31.320 --> 01:08:32.980
Exactly.

1490
01:08:32.980 --> 01:08:35.490
And I think that's what you see so much is that

1491
01:08:35.490 --> 01:08:40.370
when we do things like throw people

1492
01:08:40.370 --> 01:08:42.500
into the deep end and see if they survive,

1493
01:08:42.500 --> 01:08:45.290
we lose so many good candidates.

1494
01:08:45.290 --> 01:08:46.710
We're not developing people.

1495
01:08:46.710 --> 01:08:49.050
And this is where it also ties into the military

1496
01:08:49.050 --> 01:08:51.090
because people have taken it from there, is they say,

1497
01:08:51.090 --> 01:08:53.330
oh, the Navy Seals hell week,

1498
01:08:53.330 --> 01:08:55.760
they go through all this crazy stuff.

1499
01:08:55.760 --> 01:08:57.810
That's a sorting exercise.

1500
01:08:57.810 --> 01:08:59.680
That's not a development exercise.

1501
01:08:59.680 --> 01:09:02.420
So explain the difference between those two things.

1502
01:09:02.420 --> 01:09:04.810
So sorting is pretty simple,

1503
01:09:04.810 --> 01:09:07.670
is they're just trying to see, okay,

1504
01:09:07.670 --> 01:09:12.190
can you go through this rigors so that we can select you

1505
01:09:12.190 --> 01:09:16.210
to see if you can like handle this job?

1506
01:09:16.210 --> 01:09:17.360
So they're simulating.

1507
01:09:17.360 --> 01:09:20.150
It's literally the initial litmus test

1508
01:09:20.150 --> 01:09:23.150
to see if you're even a candidate to be developed

1509
01:09:23.150 --> 01:09:26.541
into what ultimately could be a Navy Seal.

1510
01:09:26.541 --> 01:09:28.690
It's like taking the SAT, right.

1511
01:09:28.690 --> 01:09:30.620
We're gonna create some sort of barrier

1512
01:09:30.620 --> 01:09:32.700
and this will help us.

1513
01:09:32.700 --> 01:09:33.760
But we mistook it.

1514
01:09:33.760 --> 01:09:35.823
And we took it as the development method,

1515
01:09:36.860 --> 01:09:40.560
which if you go like people, coaches, parents,

1516
01:09:40.560 --> 01:09:43.097
whoever they think, oh, well,

1517
01:09:43.097 --> 01:09:44.070
the way to develop discipline

1518
01:09:44.070 --> 01:09:46.980
and toughness is take the Navy Seals method,

1519
01:09:46.980 --> 01:09:50.990
which is put people through some really difficult things.

1520
01:09:50.990 --> 01:09:52.610
Like, and then they'll get really tough.

1521
01:09:52.610 --> 01:09:54.773
No, that's not what the Navy Seals even do.

1522
01:09:55.760 --> 01:09:58.870
If they're talking about developing toughness,

1523
01:09:58.870 --> 01:10:03.470
they focus on more of what I'd call that Eastern side.

1524
01:10:03.470 --> 01:10:05.400
As you mentioned earlier, where it's just like,

1525
01:10:05.400 --> 01:10:07.930
hey, we've gotta create space.

1526
01:10:07.930 --> 01:10:10.463
We've gotta develop the mental skills.

1527
01:10:11.856 --> 01:10:13.770
I talked to a really good athlete,

1528
01:10:13.770 --> 01:10:16.120
former college athlete of mine who went on into

1529
01:10:17.010 --> 01:10:19.460
the military and now is trying for the special forces.

1530
01:10:19.460 --> 01:10:21.570
And he put it pretty succinctly.

1531
01:10:21.570 --> 01:10:23.430
It was like, Steve,

1532
01:10:23.430 --> 01:10:26.210
before we go out and do all the crazy stuff,

1533
01:10:26.210 --> 01:10:29.403
like the survival training that we're doing,

1534
01:10:30.260 --> 01:10:35.040
we sit in a classroom, we have lecture after lecture,

1535
01:10:35.040 --> 01:10:36.510
PowerPoint after PowerPoint,

1536
01:10:36.510 --> 01:10:39.830
I've got a 600 page book filled with telling me how,

1537
01:10:39.830 --> 01:10:41.610
what to do mentally and physically

1538
01:10:41.610 --> 01:10:44.240
to handle whatever situation I'm coming at.

1539
01:10:44.240 --> 01:10:46.600
And I have to learn and understand that.

1540
01:10:46.600 --> 01:10:48.790
And it's only after I learn those skills

1541
01:10:48.790 --> 01:10:50.803
that I'm then put in a place that simulates

1542
01:10:50.803 --> 01:10:52.860
what I'll feel like and experience

1543
01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:54.700
so that I can try those skills out.

1544
01:10:54.700 --> 01:10:56.256
Right.

1545
01:10:56.256 --> 01:10:57.089
To see if you're able to access

1546
01:10:57.089 --> 01:10:59.490
that skillset under pressure and duress.

1547
01:10:59.490 --> 01:11:00.820
Exactly.

1548
01:11:00.820 --> 01:11:02.140
What's fascinating

1549
01:11:02.140 --> 01:11:06.500
is how much the military gets right

1550
01:11:06.500 --> 01:11:09.222
about this because it does dispel

1551
01:11:09.222 --> 01:11:10.570
that kind of mythic notion

1552
01:11:11.688 --> 01:11:14.490
of weeding out the week and the toughest will survive

1553
01:11:14.490 --> 01:11:17.670
and how kind of rooted in science

1554
01:11:17.670 --> 01:11:22.670
and how kind of mature and well thought out it is like

1555
01:11:22.730 --> 01:11:25.890
it is this blueprint where they've poured a ton of research

1556
01:11:25.890 --> 01:11:29.630
and money and resources into trying to figure out

1557
01:11:29.630 --> 01:11:31.670
how do you develop these skills

1558
01:11:31.670 --> 01:11:35.690
that are so critical to having a robust defense, right?

1559
01:11:35.690 --> 01:11:37.390
That is so different than what we think.

1560
01:11:37.390 --> 01:11:40.560
And it reminds me of the, I did a podcast with this guy,

1561
01:11:40.560 --> 01:11:44.370
Rich Devenney, who was basically training Navy Seals.

1562
01:11:44.370 --> 01:11:46.510
And he echoed the same thing.

1563
01:11:46.510 --> 01:11:50.160
It's like, it's all about developing mental resilience.

1564
01:11:50.160 --> 01:11:52.210
Like tho those other things are just tests

1565
01:11:52.210 --> 01:11:54.740
and they get a lot of press and they're fun to talk about,

1566
01:11:54.740 --> 01:11:57.820
but the truth is much more complicated

1567
01:11:57.820 --> 01:12:00.750
and nuanced and has to do with psychology.

1568
01:12:00.750 --> 01:12:02.850
And how do you inherently motivate somebody

1569
01:12:02.850 --> 01:12:07.130
and develop a team approach to problem solving

1570
01:12:07.130 --> 01:12:08.580
and all this kind of thing.

1571
01:12:08.580 --> 01:12:11.100
You know, I think the way I like to think

1572
01:12:11.100 --> 01:12:15.770
of it is the public image is stuck in the 1940s military.

1573
01:12:15.770 --> 01:12:20.610
