If they start thinking about what they're doing,

2609
01:57:50.870 --> 01:57:52.490
then they yeah, yeah, yeah.

2610
01:57:52.490 --> 01:57:54.920
So it keeps you in that state.

2611
01:57:54.920 --> 01:57:57.030
So if you're in flow in that state,

2612
01:57:57.030 --> 01:57:58.100
whatever works there,

2613
01:57:58.100 --> 01:58:00.120
but that's where these tools help.

2614
01:58:00.120 --> 01:58:03.340
If you have the ability to what I call zoom in and zoom out,

2615
01:58:03.340 --> 01:58:04.910
like distract yourself,

2616
01:58:04.910 --> 01:58:08.240
or really dig down and get super focused.

2617
01:58:08.240 --> 01:58:10.140
If you train that ability, when you're in flow,

2618
01:58:10.140 --> 01:58:11.790
you have the ability to distract yourself

2619
01:58:11.790 --> 01:58:13.090
a little bit productively,

2620
01:58:15.293 --> 01:58:17.090
but the other part of it is we're not

2621
01:58:17.090 --> 01:58:19.610
always gonna be able to get in flow,

2622
01:58:19.610 --> 01:58:22.420
flow generally occurs when it's,

2623
01:58:22.420 --> 01:58:24.520
what's called a just manageable challenge

2624
01:58:25.790 --> 01:58:29.463
and physiological arousal levels are moderate.

2625
01:58:30.960 --> 01:58:35.000
So if we can keep our arousal levels relatively high,

2626
01:58:35.000 --> 01:58:37.083
but within check, we can get in flow.

2627
01:58:37.980 --> 01:58:42.480
If for whatever reason, our nerves, anxiety, pressure,

2628
01:58:42.480 --> 01:58:46.480
et cetera, get our arousal levels up high, super high.

2629
01:58:46.480 --> 01:58:48.070
We can't get in flow.

2630
01:58:48.070 --> 01:58:50.240
So we need to be able to do something

2631
01:58:50.240 --> 01:58:52.720
when we're in situations where like

2632
01:58:52.720 --> 01:58:55.490
those arousal levels are through the roof,

2633
01:58:55.490 --> 01:58:56.830
and what the science and research

2634
01:58:56.830 --> 01:58:58.870
and psychology says there is that you can enter

2635
01:58:58.870 --> 01:59:00.320
what's called a clutch state,

2636
01:59:01.330 --> 01:59:02.820
which is essentially,

2637
01:59:02.820 --> 01:59:05.890
instead of flow is like letting it happen.

2638
01:59:05.890 --> 01:59:07.734
Clutch is like,

2639
01:59:07.734 --> 01:59:10.134
you have to make the decision to make it happen.

2640
01:59:11.140 --> 01:59:15.200
And there it's like, you need again, similar tools,

2641
01:59:15.200 --> 01:59:19.050
but now instead of distraction, zooming like out,

2642
01:59:19.050 --> 01:59:20.820
you have to zoom in,

2643
01:59:20.820 --> 01:59:24.634
while not losing your mind because you're super like,

2644
01:59:24.634 --> 01:59:25.720
you're adrenaline's going nuts.

2645
01:59:25.720 --> 01:59:27.480
So the clutch state also,

2646
01:59:27.480 --> 01:59:31.950
I suspect can be trained by placing yourself in stressful

2647
01:59:31.950 --> 01:59:34.540
situations and trying to recall

2648
01:59:34.540 --> 01:59:37.870
these mindfulness practices to provide space.

2649
01:59:37.870 --> 01:59:40.000
So it's something you can train for.

2650
01:59:40.000 --> 01:59:43.620
And I love the idea between the zoom in,

2651
01:59:43.620 --> 01:59:44.980
and you use like Frank Shorter

2652
01:59:44.980 --> 01:59:46.780
and marathoning versus the zoom out,

2653
01:59:46.780 --> 01:59:48.100
which is the fighter pilot

2654
01:59:48.100 --> 01:59:50.260
that has to like take into account,

2655
01:59:50.260 --> 01:59:52.410
like all these things that are going on because

2656
01:59:52.410 --> 01:59:55.140
he's confronted with all these dials and switches.

2657
01:59:55.140 --> 01:59:58.060
And he can't just focus on one thing in order

2658
01:59:58.060 --> 02:00:01.560
to make the right decision in that clutch experience.

2659
02:00:01.560 --> 02:00:02.540
Exactly.

2660
02:00:02.540 --> 02:00:05.380
That's one of the things that was really fun,

2661
02:00:05.380 --> 02:00:08.360
researching and writing about is because again,

2662
02:00:08.360 --> 02:00:09.720
there's nuance and maybe this

2663
02:00:09.720 --> 02:00:11.950
is what the message of the book

2664
02:00:11.950 --> 02:00:16.500
is like the more skills or tools you have in the tool belt,

2665
02:00:16.500 --> 02:00:18.960
the more you're able to match one of those tools

2666
02:00:18.960 --> 02:00:22.400
with whatever situation like you face.

2667
02:00:22.400 --> 02:00:25.860
So in the example, you just gave there, like Frank Shorter,

2668
02:00:25.860 --> 02:00:30.260
the marathoner, had to zoom in and get really focused.

2669
02:00:30.260 --> 02:00:32.700
If you're fighter pilot,

2670
02:00:32.700 --> 02:00:33.880
like you have to zoom out.

2671
02:00:33.880 --> 02:00:35.810
So you can pay attention to all these things.

2672
02:00:35.810 --> 02:00:38.650
Because like when danger is happening,

2673
02:00:38.650 --> 02:00:40.170
your mind tends to narrow

2674
02:00:40.170 --> 02:00:42.570
in on the like one little beeping buzzing thing.

2675
02:00:43.962 --> 02:00:46.890
And the research shows that even if something else

2676
02:00:46.890 --> 02:00:49.160
is like beeping and buzzing, you don't hear it,

2677
02:00:49.160 --> 02:00:51.100
which can be really dangerous if you're

2678
02:00:51.100 --> 02:00:55.023
in like dangerous situation in the air.

2679
02:00:56.025 --> 02:00:58.060
So you gotta train your mind to zoom back out,

2680
02:00:58.060 --> 02:00:59.600
even though it's telling you,

2681
02:00:59.600 --> 02:01:02.900
like focus on this one dial that is wrong.

2682
02:01:02.900 --> 02:01:03.950
That's fascinating.

2683
02:01:05.845 --> 02:01:08.010
Because if you're thinking about marathoning,

2684
02:01:08.010 --> 02:01:09.500
like just focus on your breath,

2685
02:01:09.500 --> 02:01:12.020
like pick one thing as a metronome

2686
02:01:12.020 --> 02:01:15.090
to crowd out all of the pain,

2687
02:01:15.090 --> 02:01:17.490
and whatever other things that can be distraction.

2688
02:01:17.490 --> 02:01:20.630
So you can just execute on the one thing.

2689
02:01:20.630 --> 02:01:24.670
But that's very different from a rapidly developing

2690
02:01:24.670 --> 02:01:26.500
and potentially dangerous situation

2691
02:01:26.500 --> 02:01:29.650
where you're being attacked by lots of different things.

2692
02:01:29.650 --> 02:01:33.110
And you have to do some really rapid mind work

2693
02:01:33.110 --> 02:01:36.570
to figure out where your attention is best placed.

2694
02:01:36.570 --> 02:01:37.660
Exactly.

2695
02:01:37.660 --> 02:01:39.650
Attention is a tool.

2696
02:01:39.650 --> 02:01:41.430
It's a trainable tool.

2697
02:01:41.430 --> 02:01:45.193
And I think too often, we just kind of let it happen,

2698
02:01:46.370 --> 02:01:49.507
but training it happens when you're deliberate about it.

2699
02:01:49.507 --> 02:01:51.630
So how do you train it to know when you need

2700
02:01:51.630 --> 02:01:53.370
to zoom in and when you need to zoom out?

2701
02:01:53.370 --> 02:01:55.430
So you're not just pray to whatever

2702
02:01:55.430 --> 02:01:57.833
your brain wants to do, right?

2703
02:01:59.680 --> 02:02:00.530
Yes.

2704
02:02:00.530 --> 02:02:04.490
So here, I think this comes down to like practice, right.

2705
02:02:04.490 --> 02:02:06.920
Is to use the fighter example.

2706
02:02:06.920 --> 02:02:08.119
What do they do?

2707
02:02:08.119 --> 02:02:09.720
They go in simulators, right.

2708
02:02:09.720 --> 02:02:11.340
And they have all these alarms buzzing

2709
02:02:11.340 --> 02:02:12.790
and beeping, et cetera.

2710
02:02:12.790 --> 02:02:13.880
And they have someone watching

2711
02:02:13.880 --> 02:02:16.160
and essentially coaching them up.

2712
02:02:16.160 --> 02:02:19.260
And if you miss an alarm, what does that tell you?

2713
02:02:19.260 --> 02:02:21.837
You needed to zoom out in that situation

2714
02:02:21.837 --> 02:02:23.420
and you didn't because you didn't hear

2715
02:02:23.420 --> 02:02:24.380
that other alarm there.

2716
02:02:24.380 --> 02:02:25.810
But what if you're zoomed out

2717
02:02:25.810 --> 02:02:27.310
and you're just hearing lots of bells

2718
02:02:27.310 --> 02:02:28.770
and you're just taking it all in

2719
02:02:28.770 --> 02:02:31.200
and what you really needed to do was figure

2720
02:02:31.200 --> 02:02:33.190
out how to zoom in on the right thing.

2721
02:02:33.190 --> 02:02:35.040
That's why they have the checklist.

2722
02:02:36.925 --> 02:02:38.350
I mean, that's the reason,

2723
02:02:38.350 --> 02:02:41.000
but I think in other pursuits,

2724
02:02:41.000 --> 02:02:45.760
it's when I'm coaching runners, what often happens,

2725
02:02:45.760 --> 02:02:49.500
and I did some research on this actually is what we focus on

2726
02:02:49.500 --> 02:02:51.190
in practice is entirely different

2727
02:02:51.190 --> 02:02:53.570
than what we focus on in a race.

2728
02:02:53.570 --> 02:02:56.270
So in practice, we know we're gonna make through it.

2729
02:02:56.270 --> 02:02:58.623
We know we're gonna get on another side.

2730
02:02:58.623 --> 02:03:00.130
It's challenging,

2731
02:03:00.130 --> 02:03:02.930
but it's no one's watching it doesn't matter.

2732
02:03:02.930 --> 02:03:05.160
So what we tend to think of is, yeah,

2733
02:03:05.160 --> 02:03:07.900
we're focused on the work, but we'll think of,

2734
02:03:07.900 --> 02:03:10.380
oh, what am I gonna have for lunch?

2735
02:03:10.380 --> 02:03:12.930
What am I doing later with Johnny or Susie?

2736
02:03:12.930 --> 02:03:14.560
Like, there's more distracting thoughts

2737
02:03:14.560 --> 02:03:17.773
of that stuff, in a race there's almost none of that.

2738
02:03:18.660 --> 02:03:19.493
So in practice,

2739
02:03:19.493 --> 02:03:21.980
we're training our brain to like get through this workout

2740
02:03:21.980 --> 02:03:24.160
by sometimes occasionally thinking about

2741
02:03:24.160 --> 02:03:26.060
like the future and distracting us,

2742
02:03:26.060 --> 02:03:29.010
but that's not what we're facing in a race.

2743
02:03:29.010 --> 02:03:31.690
So instead of seeing practice just as,

2744
02:03:31.690 --> 02:03:33.830
hey, I need to hit these times

2745
02:03:33.830 --> 02:03:36.190
on this workout to get things done.

2746
02:03:36.190 --> 02:03:37.530
You need to see it as what's

2747
02:03:37.530 --> 02:03:40.540
my psychological or mental goal.

2748
02:03:40.540 --> 02:03:42.740
What tool am I training?

2749
02:03:42.740 --> 02:03:45.530
And actually one of my really good athletes in college,

2750
02:03:45.530 --> 02:03:48.020
Brian Brazos, went on to run professionally.

2751
02:03:48.020 --> 02:03:50.470
He called it some practices

2752
02:03:50.470 --> 02:03:54.130
I need to let my mind go to a bad place

2753
02:03:54.130 --> 02:03:57.040
and then see if I can figure my way out.

2754
02:03:57.040 --> 02:04:00.390
And if I can't, if I fall apart,

2755
02:04:00.390 --> 02:04:02.990
then that tells me that I didn't have the right tools

2756
02:04:02.990 --> 02:04:05.390
to use and I've gotta develop those tools.

2757
02:04:05.390 --> 02:04:07.910
So an example of that would be what,

2758
02:04:07.910 --> 02:04:09.930
like let's say you're doing, I don't know,

2759
02:04:09.930 --> 02:04:11.340
400 repeats or whatever,

2760
02:04:11.340 --> 02:04:14.700
like just run the first hundred meters way too fast.

2761
02:04:14.700 --> 02:04:15.590
So then you're like

2762
02:04:15.590 --> 02:04:17.430
you would never do that in a race,

2763
02:04:17.430 --> 02:04:19.770
but what would I do in this situation

2764
02:04:19.770 --> 02:04:22.220
where I've overexerted too early on.

2765
02:04:22.220 --> 02:04:23.580
Put yourself in a hole.

2766
02:04:23.580 --> 02:04:25.152
Exactly.

2767
02:04:25.152 --> 02:04:26.230
Like you put yourself in a hole

2768
02:04:26.230 --> 02:04:28.210
and then now you have to like,

2769
02:04:28.210 --> 02:04:30.610
get out of that because like,

2770
02:04:30.610 --> 02:04:31.550
you can't just quit.

2771
02:04:31.550 --> 02:04:32.930
You're just not gonna stop.

2772
02:04:32.930 --> 02:04:34.930
So like, how do you get out of that situation?

2773
02:04:34.930 --> 02:04:37.573
Which is not comfortable.

2774
02:04:39.040 --> 02:04:40.800
Yeah, Interesting.

2775
02:04:40.800 --> 02:04:42.250
Well, let's talk about the last one,

2776
02:04:42.250 --> 02:04:46.000
which is the last pillar, transcending discomfort.

2777
02:04:46.000 --> 02:04:48.500
I mean, this is sort of like the global thing, right?

2778
02:04:48.500 --> 02:04:51.590
Like how can you inoculate yourself

2779
02:04:51.590 --> 02:04:52.910
or that's the wrong word.

2780
02:04:52.910 --> 02:04:56.700
How can you just create a habit

2781
02:04:59.062 --> 02:05:01.640
of exposing yourself to difficult things

2782
02:05:01.640 --> 02:05:03.430
and increasing that level

2783
02:05:03.430 --> 02:05:05.633
of difficulty gradually over time?

2784
02:05:06.550 --> 02:05:11.550
So this was probably one of the funnest sections

2785
02:05:12.080 --> 02:05:14.020
or the funnest section,

2786
02:05:14.020 --> 02:05:16.600
because it like pulls out from the individual and says,

2787
02:05:16.600 --> 02:05:18.750
okay, what's the global thing, as you said,

2788
02:05:21.596 --> 02:05:25.100
and it's kind of like surprising, but it kind of isn't,

2789
02:05:25.100 --> 02:05:29.250
but it's like, well, if you fulfill

2790
02:05:29.250 --> 02:05:33.870
people's basic psychological needs and you combine

2791
02:05:33.870 --> 02:05:38.493
that with meaning people can handle really difficult things.

2792
02:05:39.630 --> 02:05:42.230
And what are those basic psychological needs?

2793
02:05:42.230 --> 02:05:45.110
Competency, feeling like I can make progress

2794
02:05:45.110 --> 02:05:47.200
and grow in whatever I'm doing,

2795
02:05:47.200 --> 02:05:49.280
autonomy, which we talked about earlier,

2796
02:05:49.280 --> 02:05:51.490
like feeling like I have some sort of control

2797
02:05:51.490 --> 02:05:55.840
over things and belonging, do I belong to this group?

2798
02:05:55.840 --> 02:05:59.380
Do I feel a part of this, is this bigger than myself?

2799
02:05:59.380 --> 02:06:01.890
And you combine that with meaning,

2800
02:06:01.890 --> 02:06:05.810
meaning is this endeavor or this thing

2801
02:06:05.810 --> 02:06:09.070
I'm getting through that is really hard.

2802
02:06:09.070 --> 02:06:11.200
Is it more than just for a paycheck

2803
02:06:11.200 --> 02:06:13.463
or for whatever it.

2804
02:06:15.160 --> 02:06:18.660
So as a coach or as a leader in the workplace,

2805
02:06:18.660 --> 02:06:22.760
like how do you cultivate those things

2806
02:06:22.760 --> 02:06:27.100
so that your team can do the best they can do?

2807
02:06:27.100 --> 02:06:30.080
So I think this is where we often get it wrong.

2808
02:06:30.080 --> 02:06:31.480
What do we do in the workplace?

2809
02:06:31.480 --> 02:06:34.023
We often come up with core values and slogans.

2810
02:06:34.900 --> 02:06:36.200
We put them on the wall.

2811
02:06:38.055 --> 02:06:40.053
And then we say, look at those things.

2812
02:06:40.053 --> 02:06:43.350
With a picture of like a crew rowing a boat.

2813
02:06:43.350 --> 02:06:45.183
Yeah, exactly.

2814
02:06:45.183 --> 02:06:46.483
It's like the stock photo.

2815
02:06:47.320 --> 02:06:48.990
And it's like, look at these things.

2816
02:06:48.990 --> 02:06:51.020
So motive, so inspired.

2817
02:06:51.020 --> 02:06:52.650
This is the problem.

2818
02:06:52.650 --> 02:06:54.260
They're not authentic.

2819
02:06:54.260 --> 02:06:56.220
People know, right.

2820
02:06:56.220 --> 02:07:01.220
People like understand our brains can tell real from fake.

2821
02:07:01.690 --> 02:07:05.630
So if it's not authentic, it's not going to work.

2822
02:07:05.630 --> 02:07:08.070
So the best thing that you can do as a leader

2823
02:07:08.070 --> 02:07:12.580
to create resilient teams is to be authentic

2824
02:07:12.580 --> 02:07:15.350
and support people in a way that helps

2825
02:07:15.350 --> 02:07:17.650
them be authentic in what they're doing.

2826
02:07:17.650 --> 02:07:22.140
So if you're talking about autonomy, belonging,

2827
02:07:22.140 --> 02:07:24.050
all of these things, well,

2828
02:07:24.050 --> 02:07:28.410
you have to actually want those, number one.

2829
02:07:28.410 --> 02:07:29.330
And then number two,

2830
02:07:29.330 --> 02:07:33.420
you have to lead by example by setting the stage.

2831
02:07:33.420 --> 02:07:35.560
And I think there's some brilliant examples in sport.

2832
02:07:35.560 --> 02:07:38.170
One that I talk about in the book is

2833
02:07:38.170 --> 02:07:42.320
Greg Popovich, the famous coach for the San Antonio Spurs,

2834
02:07:42.320 --> 02:07:44.690
how does he create belonging?

2835
02:07:44.690 --> 02:07:47.600
Well, before getting there, actually I'll tell a story.

2836
02:07:47.600 --> 02:07:49.190
Then I'll get to there.

2837
02:07:49.190 --> 02:07:51.600
He creates belonging by

2838
02:07:51.600 --> 02:07:55.120
inviting all the players to these like,

2839
02:07:55.120 --> 02:07:58.450
well thought out dinners where he takes

2840
02:07:58.450 --> 02:08:00.810
the time to pick the food.

2841
02:08:00.810 --> 02:08:02.280
He's a wine expert.

2842
02:08:02.280 --> 02:08:04.080
He picks the wine,

2843
02:08:04.080 --> 02:08:07.140
he arranges the tables in the right way

2844
02:08:07.140 --> 02:08:09.070
with the right people to kind of create

2845
02:08:09.070 --> 02:08:10.930
this natural conversation.

2846
02:08:10.930 --> 02:08:12.340
'Cause how do we create belonging?

2847
02:08:12.340 --> 02:08:17.160
It's not through like trust falls and like random exercises.

2848
02:08:17.160 --> 02:08:19.550
It's through genuine connection.

2849
02:08:19.550 --> 02:08:22.150
What Popovich does brilliantly is he creates

2850
02:08:22.150 --> 02:08:24.570
the space for genuine connection.

2851
02:08:24.570 --> 02:08:26.530
And it's something that he's passionate about.

2852
02:08:26.530 --> 02:08:28.220
He loves food, he loves wine.

2853
02:08:28.220 --> 02:08:29.790
So people get excited about it.

2854
02:08:29.790 --> 02:08:32.000
But he doesn't let them choose the wine or the food.

2855
02:08:32.000 --> 02:08:32.873
No, he doesn't.

2856
02:08:33.720 --> 02:08:34.870
That's true.

2857
02:08:34.870 --> 02:08:37.520
But here here's what happened.

2858
02:08:37.520 --> 02:08:40.260
I've worked with a couple of NBA teams

2859
02:08:40.260 --> 02:08:43.610
and after this kind of story broke, they'd be like,

2860
02:08:43.610 --> 02:08:47.423
Steve, we tried team dinners, no one showed up.

2861
02:08:48.280 --> 02:08:51.210
They'd be like, you're missing the picture,

2862
02:08:51.210 --> 02:08:52.710
the dinner isn't the thing.

2863
02:08:52.710 --> 02:08:54.700
It can be an avenue.

2864
02:08:54.700 --> 02:08:55.550
But the thing is,

2865
02:08:55.550 --> 02:08:57.930
Popovich was really passionate about this

2866
02:08:57.930 --> 02:08:59.940
and created the environment where people wanted

2867
02:08:59.940 --> 02:09:01.523
to be and wanted to connect.

2868
02:09:02.700 --> 02:09:05.270
'Cause it was authentic to what he was inspired by.

2869
02:09:05.270 --> 02:09:06.460
Exactly.

2870
02:09:06.460 --> 02:09:10.330
Like find that thing for you, whatever it is.

2871
02:09:10.330 --> 02:09:14.420
And it will like coalesce and help people.

2872
02:09:14.420 --> 02:09:19.420
I'm just imagining the chief financial officer

2873
02:09:20.220 --> 02:09:23.463
who's into birding making his team go birding with him.

2874
02:09:25.970 --> 02:09:27.400
It might go sideways.

2875
02:09:27.400 --> 02:09:28.750
It might go sideways.

2876
02:09:28.750 --> 02:09:30.640
You know, it has to be something people

2877
02:09:30.640 --> 02:09:32.390
generally like to do.

2878
02:09:32.390 --> 02:09:35.960
But I do think it's like that passion

2879
02:09:35.960 --> 02:09:38.800
is contagious and opens up an avenue.

2880
02:09:38.800 --> 02:09:41.580
But the bottom line is it's again, very simple.

2881
02:09:41.580 --> 02:09:42.750
It's be authentic.

2882
02:09:42.750 --> 02:09:44.810
And the other thing that I think is important

2883
02:09:44.810 --> 02:09:46.151
and that I've found in the research

2884
02:09:46.151 --> 02:09:48.980
is that we often think that

2885
02:09:51.477 --> 02:09:53.296
we have to like establish trust

2886
02:09:53.296 --> 02:09:56.450
and then be authentic and vulnerable.

2887
02:09:56.450 --> 02:09:57.503
But it's opposite.

2888
02:09:58.428 --> 02:10:01.188
Authenticity breeds trust.
Exactly.

2889
02:10:01.188 --> 02:10:06.188
Is you don't get that trust without the authenticity.

2890
02:10:06.600 --> 02:10:09.280
You have to take the leap of faith so that

2891
02:10:09.280 --> 02:10:12.820
the person sitting across from you says, oh man,

2892
02:10:12.820 --> 02:10:16.070
like that person is taking that leap.

2893
02:10:16.070 --> 02:10:17.200
I'm gonna do that with them.

2894
02:10:17.200 --> 02:10:19.520
And authenticity demands vulnerability.

2895
02:10:19.520 --> 02:10:22.810
And this is where it gets tricky because leaders

2896
02:10:22.810 --> 02:10:26.640
and athletes have been a nerd to that idea.

2897
02:10:26.640 --> 02:10:28.260
Like we've been taught since the beginning,

2898
02:10:28.260 --> 02:10:29.990
don't show weakness, right.

2899
02:10:29.990 --> 02:10:32.360
Where ultimately greater strength

2900
02:10:32.360 --> 02:10:35.470
is mined through the exploration of vulnerability,

2901
02:10:35.470 --> 02:10:37.410
but it's a counterintuitive principle

2902
02:10:37.410 --> 02:10:40.710
for a lot of leaders and high achievers.

2903
02:10:40.710 --> 02:10:41.640
Exactly.

2904
02:10:41.640 --> 02:10:43.697
And I think the other part is,

2905
02:10:43.697 --> 02:10:46.540
is it's like once you've, if you're a high achiever,

2906
02:10:46.540 --> 02:10:49.430
you're a leader, you're a CEO.

2907
02:10:49.430 --> 02:10:51.290
Like you've been taught those things.

2908
02:10:51.290 --> 02:10:54.200
And you've also kind of grown into this hierarchy

2909
02:10:54.200 --> 02:10:56.610
where it's almost like you have

2910
02:10:56.610 --> 02:10:59.080
to learn how to put your ego aside as well,

2911
02:10:59.080 --> 02:11:01.280
which is a very difficult thing.

2912
02:11:01.280 --> 02:11:03.400
Because you're known as the CEO,

2913
02:11:03.400 --> 02:11:06.373
this guy who has the answers, all this good stuff.

2914
02:11:07.270 --> 02:11:08.747
And now you have to say

2915
02:11:08.747 --> 02:11:12.050
and I'm gonna quiet down my ego so

2916
02:11:12.050 --> 02:11:14.060
that like we can share things

2917
02:11:14.060 --> 02:11:17.660
that might be real and difficult and hard.

2918
02:11:17.660 --> 02:11:19.300
Because the delusion is that

2919
02:11:19.300 --> 02:11:21.780
by expressing that vulnerability,

2920
02:11:21.780 --> 02:11:24.210
that you'll degrade the trust that you've created.

2921
02:11:24.210 --> 02:11:25.060
But ultimately,

2922
02:11:25.060 --> 02:11:28.130
as we just talked about, that authentic expression

2923
02:11:28.130 --> 02:11:31.960
of vulnerability will engender even greater trust.

2924
02:11:31.960 --> 02:11:32.917
Exactly.

2925
02:11:32.917 --> 02:11:36.240
But it's a risk, it's scary.

2926
02:11:36.240 --> 02:11:37.073
It is.

2927
02:11:37.073 --> 02:11:40.330
And that's where I call this book "Do Hard Things",

2928
02:11:40.330 --> 02:11:42.600
because I think part of it is,

2929
02:11:42.600 --> 02:11:45.440
and you talked about this even with myself and running

2930
02:11:45.440 --> 02:11:50.440
is that the growth comes from the discomfort, it's in there.

2931
02:11:52.200 --> 02:11:53.800
If you can go in there,

2932
02:11:53.800 --> 02:11:57.790
navigate it and figure out how to come out on other side.

2933
02:11:57.790 --> 02:12:00.120
And the beautiful thing is you see this cover

2934
02:12:00.120 --> 02:12:01.260
and you're like "Do Hard Things",

2935
02:12:01.260 --> 02:12:03.140
like you think you're gonna read this book about like,

2936
02:12:03.140 --> 02:12:06.580
here's how I'm gonna go to Seal camp and make it through.

2937
02:12:06.580 --> 02:12:10.430
And it's like, no, for any athlete,

2938
02:12:10.430 --> 02:12:12.380
they already know like doing

2939
02:12:12.380 --> 02:12:13.850
the hard thing is the easy thing

2940
02:12:13.850 --> 02:12:16.530
when it comes to the practicality of the training,

2941
02:12:16.530 --> 02:12:18.450
if anything, we need to hold ourselves back.

2942
02:12:18.450 --> 02:12:21.600
Like that's not the issue that needs throttling.

2943
02:12:21.600 --> 02:12:23.290
The real hard thing,

2944
02:12:23.290 --> 02:12:26.910
the hard thing that we need to do is the more nuanced

2945
02:12:28.180 --> 02:12:32.190
approach to all of this that requires these challenging

2946
02:12:32.190 --> 02:12:34.990
and frightening emotions to be called forth,

2947
02:12:34.990 --> 02:12:38.300
such as vulnerability and authenticity and mindfulness

2948
02:12:38.300 --> 02:12:39.930
and all of this kind of thing.

2949
02:12:39.930 --> 02:12:41.267
Exactly, yeah.

2950
02:12:41.267 --> 02:12:42.610
The things that we think are hard,

2951
02:12:42.610 --> 02:12:44.010
aren't actually the hard thing.

2952
02:12:44.010 --> 02:12:47.483
That's the succinct way of putting what I just said.

2953
02:12:49.000 --> 02:12:50.430
Yeah, exactly.

2954
02:12:50.430 --> 02:12:53.120
So it's kind of like a Trojan horse in that regard.

2955
02:12:53.120 --> 02:12:53.970
100%.

2956
02:12:53.970 --> 02:12:55.030
Yeah. Yeah.

2957
02:12:55.030 --> 02:12:56.403
Well, the book is amazing.

2958
02:12:57.410 --> 02:12:58.570
Before I let you go, though,

2959
02:12:58.570 --> 02:13:00.480
there are like a couple things

2960
02:13:00.480 --> 02:13:01.650
that I wanted to ask you about.

2961
02:13:01.650 --> 02:13:02.850
Not the least, because it's sort

2962
02:13:02.850 --> 02:13:05.730
of been top of news right now,

2963
02:13:05.730 --> 02:13:09.380
which is the fact that this six year old ran

2964
02:13:09.380 --> 02:13:13.010
a marathon last week and the internet is not

2965
02:13:14.610 --> 02:13:18.513
speaking kindly of what just occurred.

2966
02:13:19.611 --> 02:13:21.980
Is that the youngest person

2967
02:13:21.980 --> 02:13:23.870
who's ever done a sanctioned marathon?

2968
02:13:23.870 --> 02:13:24.920
I think it is, yeah.

2969
02:13:24.920 --> 02:13:25.753
That's the youngest one.

2970
02:13:25.753 --> 02:13:26.760
What's going on here?

2971
02:13:27.820 --> 02:13:32.320
You know, I'm not the parent, but I can tell you,

2972
02:13:32.320 --> 02:13:34.573
and I think Kara Goucher said this very well,

2973
02:13:36.000 --> 02:13:38.410
this isn't gonna help the child.

2974
02:13:38.410 --> 02:13:40.660
It didn't seem like, and I don't know.

2975
02:13:40.660 --> 02:13:42.520
And I didn't dive too deeply into this,

2976
02:13:42.520 --> 02:13:44.130
but it does seem like the kid

2977
02:13:44.130 --> 02:13:46.300
wasn't really that enthusiastic.

2978
02:13:46.300 --> 02:13:49.180
It's not like it was intrinsically driven

2979
02:13:49.180 --> 02:13:51.250
by this kid's desire to do this.

2980
02:13:51.250 --> 02:13:53.300
It was more coming from the parents.

2981
02:13:53.300 --> 02:13:57.230
So if you look at it, the parents are runners.

2982
02:13:57.230 --> 02:13:59.120
They have this thing where they

2983
02:13:59.120 --> 02:14:01.460
do all these races with their,

2984
02:14:01.460 --> 02:14:02.780
I think they have six children.

2985
02:14:02.780 --> 02:14:05.067
They do the Appalachian Trail or something.

2986
02:14:05.067 --> 02:14:07.100
Yeah, so it's a running family,

2987
02:14:07.100 --> 02:14:09.210
et cetera, which is fine.

2988
02:14:09.210 --> 02:14:10.150
Whatever.

2989
02:14:10.150 --> 02:14:12.193
But if you're a six year old,

2990
02:14:13.499 --> 02:14:15.220
you have to put your mind in that six year old,

2991
02:14:15.220 --> 02:14:17.340
a six year old doesn't have the choice.

2992
02:14:17.340 --> 02:14:19.550
They don't know what they're getting into,

2993
02:14:19.550 --> 02:14:21.080
what they probably See,

2994
02:14:21.080 --> 02:14:24.430
and it took them like eight hours to run the marathon.

2995
02:14:24.430 --> 02:14:25.950
So they obviously weren't trained,

2996
02:14:25.950 --> 02:14:28.330
although I don't know how you'd train a six year old,

2997
02:14:28.330 --> 02:14:32.930
but what you see here is most likely is pretty simple.

2998
02:14:32.930 --> 02:14:34.500
You have a kid who sees all

2999
02:14:34.500 --> 02:14:37.950
his siblings participating in these races.

3000
02:14:37.950 --> 02:14:41.160
You have parents who participate

3001
02:14:41.160 --> 02:14:42.700
in these races with the family

3002
02:14:42.700 --> 02:14:46.910
and video them for YouTube and all that stuff.

3003
02:14:46.910 --> 02:14:50.300
And you just have a kid who probably wants attention,

3004
02:14:50.300 --> 02:14:55.300
support, love, et cetera, like any child wants.

3005
02:14:55.890 --> 02:14:59.080
And the path towards that looked

3006
02:14:59.080 --> 02:15:00.890
like probably running a marathon.

3007
02:15:00.890 --> 02:15:02.440
Right, it doesn't necessitate

3008
02:15:03.283 --> 02:15:04.760
an explicit pronouncement from the parent.

3009
02:15:04.760 --> 02:15:06.140
Like we expect you to do this.

3010
02:15:06.140 --> 02:15:09.500
It's just implied because this is what we do as a family.

3011
02:15:09.500 --> 02:15:13.890
Exactly, and that's where I think it it's really dangerous

3012
02:15:13.890 --> 02:15:18.890
because like, again, as a parent, you have to be like,

3013
02:15:19.573 --> 02:15:22.212
and I'm not one, so I'm not trying to speak for any,

3014
02:15:22.212 --> 02:15:26.500
but I've seen and worked with athletes who had similar,

3015
02:15:26.500 --> 02:15:31.203
very young experiences in running and endurance sport.

3016
02:15:32.360 --> 02:15:34.820
And it creates this weird kind

3017
02:15:35.729 --> 02:15:37.830
of identity cementation around things.

3018
02:15:37.830 --> 02:15:38.803
And this weird,

3019
02:15:39.691 --> 02:15:42.420
like this is how I get approval from my parents.

3020
02:15:42.420 --> 02:15:44.180
And this is where that comes from.

3021
02:15:44.180 --> 02:15:46.990
And that's a very dangerous game to play.

3022
02:15:46.990 --> 02:15:49.810
We're not even talking about the physical ramifications

3023
02:15:49.810 --> 02:15:52.190
of a six year old running a marathon.

3024
02:15:52.190 --> 02:15:55.080
I just think from a psychological standpoint,

3025
02:15:55.080 --> 02:15:57.740
that's a very dangerous game to play.

3026
02:15:57.740 --> 02:16:01.640
And I think it's concerning and you're seeing more

3027
02:16:01.640 --> 02:16:04.220
and more of it in the age of social media,

3028
02:16:04.220 --> 02:16:06.040
Instagram, et cetera,

3029
02:16:06.040 --> 02:16:11.040
where parents don't realize the expectations,

3030
02:16:11.910 --> 02:16:14.720
the psychological baggage they're placing

3031
02:16:14.720 --> 02:16:18.910
on their young kids from like projecting,

3032
02:16:20.470 --> 02:16:24.720
using their children for their athletic pursuits.

3033
02:16:24.720 --> 02:16:27.566
And I think it's important to point out that,

3034
02:16:27.566 --> 02:16:32.566
that nobody's implying a nefarious motive

3035
02:16:33.370 --> 02:16:34.910
on behalf of the parents.

3036
02:16:34.910 --> 02:16:37.370
It's just the lack of awareness around this.

3037
02:16:37.370 --> 02:16:42.130
Exactly, you see this at every sport,

3038
02:16:42.130 --> 02:16:44.670
you see it in the little leagues, like soccer parents,

3039
02:16:44.670 --> 02:16:46.463
or what have you, baseball parents.

3040
02:16:47.720 --> 02:16:48.680
I see it all the time.

3041
02:16:48.680 --> 02:16:51.550
As I said, my wife's a elementary school teacher

3042
02:16:51.550 --> 02:16:53.790
and I get invited to these games with her

3043
02:16:53.790 --> 02:16:56.020
and she drags me along to her classes.

3044
02:16:56.020 --> 02:16:57.960
And it's the wildest crap I've ever seen.

3045
02:16:57.960 --> 02:16:59.000
It drives me nuts.

3046
02:16:59.000 --> 02:17:00.700
Just you mean like screaming parents?

3047
02:17:00.700 --> 02:17:01.670
Screaming parents,

3048
02:17:01.670 --> 02:17:04.480
parents of like five year olds yelling at refs.

3049
02:17:04.480 --> 02:17:06.240
And you're just like,

3050
02:17:06.240 --> 02:17:09.730
it's a youth soccer like or youth tag football game,

3051
02:17:09.730 --> 02:17:10.910
like chill out.

3052
02:17:10.910 --> 02:17:12.700
But you know, one of the interesting thing

3053
02:17:12.700 --> 02:17:15.750
I was talking to a former, well teammate of mine.

3054
02:17:15.750 --> 02:17:19.710
We ran on the same club team, Lindsay Gallo,

3055
02:17:19.710 --> 02:17:23.500
who was NCAA champion at Michigan in the 1500.

3056
02:17:23.500 --> 02:17:24.950
And she made this nice comment.

3057
02:17:24.950 --> 02:17:29.079
She said, "You know what, Steve, I have kids now.

3058
02:17:29.079 --> 02:17:30.520
I have a bunch of friends

3059
02:17:30.520 --> 02:17:34.363
who used to run college at a high level.

3060
02:17:35.670 --> 02:17:38.750
They're on the sideline and they're chilled out.

3061
02:17:38.750 --> 02:17:40.570
They're not yelling at their kids.

3062
02:17:40.570 --> 02:17:43.947
They're not worried if the kid wins or loses or whatever."

3063
02:17:44.850 --> 02:17:47.370
And we had this nice conversation where it's like,

3064
02:17:47.370 --> 02:17:49.920
I've seen the same thing because if you've been through

3065
02:17:49.920 --> 02:17:53.680
the crucible, you've been to a relatively high level.

3066
02:17:53.680 --> 02:17:54.910
You know what it takes,

3067
02:17:54.910 --> 02:17:57.750
you know what the psychology is,

3068
02:17:57.750 --> 02:18:00.630
you know that like basically,

3069
02:18:00.630 --> 02:18:04.390
and this is me coming from me in coaching,

3070
02:18:04.390 --> 02:18:08.530
the parent can either get in the way or they

3071
02:18:08.530 --> 02:18:12.080
can just kind of support and allow them to flourish.

3072
02:18:12.080 --> 02:18:14.450
And so many think like,

3073
02:18:14.450 --> 02:18:15.990
oh, if I do this, this and this,

3074
02:18:15.990 --> 02:18:16.823
they get in the way.

3075
02:18:16.823 --> 02:18:19.560
But I'll tell you from a college coaching standpoint,

3076
02:18:19.560 --> 02:18:22.486
I had more than a handful of parents

3077
02:18:22.486 --> 02:18:23.319
and they're good people.

3078
02:18:23.319 --> 02:18:24.420
It's not that they're bad people.

3079
02:18:24.420 --> 02:18:26.360
They want the best for their kid.

3080
02:18:26.360 --> 02:18:29.150
I had more than a handful of parents who I had to tell,

3081
02:18:29.150 --> 02:18:32.363
hey, can you not show up at the conference championship?

3082
02:18:33.820 --> 02:18:36.490
Because the kid would freak out

3083
02:18:36.490 --> 02:18:37.810
because they have expectations.

3084
02:18:37.810 --> 02:18:40.310
And it's not like the parent was intentionally doing that.

3085
02:18:40.310 --> 02:18:41.143
No, no, no, no.

3086
02:18:41.143 --> 02:18:43.040
I get it. Yeah. Yeah.

3087
02:18:43.040 --> 02:18:45.227
It's pretty crazy.
It's wild.

3088
02:18:45.227 --> 02:18:47.113
And it is funny just being,

3089
02:18:47.113 --> 02:18:49.050
when you become a parent and you go to all those events,

3090
02:18:49.050 --> 02:18:52.970
like yeah, if you've come up in athletics,

3091
02:18:52.970 --> 02:18:55.110
particularly athletics at a high level,

3092
02:18:55.110 --> 02:18:56.840
like you're just not the person doing that.

3093
02:18:56.840 --> 02:18:59.530
Unless you had a very unhealthy experience with that.

3094
02:18:59.530 --> 02:19:03.520
The people that are screaming and yelling tend to be people

3095
02:19:03.520 --> 02:19:07.810
who were not athletes or had a very frustrated experience

3096
02:19:07.810 --> 02:19:10.390
with athletics and are projecting onto their kids

3097
02:19:10.390 --> 02:19:13.970
some kind of unfulfilled hope and dream.

3098
02:19:13.970 --> 02:19:14.803
Exactly.

3099
02:19:14.803 --> 02:19:17.400
If I had one message to parents,

3100
02:19:17.400 --> 02:19:19.660
it would be chill out.

3101
02:19:19.660 --> 02:19:20.710
Like on your athlete.

3102
02:19:21.640 --> 02:19:23.740
And I have this debate with my wife all the time,

3103
02:19:23.740 --> 02:19:26.542
who was also a very, she was all American

3104
02:19:26.542 --> 02:19:30.130
in track, in college.

3105
02:19:30.130 --> 02:19:31.749
And we have this debate all the time.

3106
02:19:31.749 --> 02:19:35.780
We're like, do we really want our kid to be like a runner

3107
02:19:35.780 --> 02:19:38.650
or not just because it's like,

3108
02:19:38.650 --> 02:19:40.430
do you want that like expectation

3109
02:19:40.430 --> 02:19:42.414
and how would we handle that?

3110
02:19:42.414 --> 02:19:45.720
And we're always just like, hands off, like no coaching,

3111
02:19:45.720 --> 02:19:48.047
like you do you like, whatever you wanna do,

3112
02:19:48.047 --> 02:19:49.180
you wanna run, great.

3113
02:19:49.180 --> 02:19:52.600
You want to play soccer or join math club doesn't matter.

3114
02:19:52.600 --> 02:19:53.920
And I think that's 'cause we've both

3115
02:19:53.920 --> 02:19:55.870
like been through that crucible of like,

3116
02:19:55.870 --> 02:19:58.820
ah, like, it's great, don't get me wrong,

3117
02:19:58.820 --> 02:20:01.380
but you've gotta be the one who chooses to do it.

3118
02:20:01.380 --> 02:20:03.410
Well, let me put you at ease a little bit

3119
02:20:03.410 --> 02:20:05.930
as somebody who's been in the parenting game for a while.

3120
02:20:05.930 --> 02:20:10.710
Like your decision as to whether or not you want your kid,

3121
02:20:10.710 --> 02:20:14.870
if you end up having a kid to go into running is really not

3122
